review_comments.body:
The PySocketSockObject can be `NULL` if the `PySSLSocket`  was created by `_SSLContext._wrap_bio`. In that case, NULL is not an error.

We could do:

```
PySocketSockObject *sock = get_socket(self, __FILE__, __LINE__);
if (sock == NULL && PyErr_Occurred()) {
    return NULL;
}
```

But we've been avoiding that sort of pattern in new code in favor of explicit error return codes.

Alternatively, we could do something like:

```
    PySocketSockObject *sock = NULL;
    if (self->Socket) {
        sock = get_socket(self, __FILE__, __LINE__);
        if (sock == NULL) {
            return NULL;
        }
    }
```

But I'm not sure that's any better than what's in the PR.
